WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
On today's AI briefing, we think Huang's exit is a governance shock, not yet a supply shock.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.780
Chips keep shipping under an interim CEO.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.400
This is the AI briefing for Thursday, November 12.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.500
I'm Michael.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.340
Like everything here, this briefing is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.480
Today, the man who built NVIDIA is out.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:33.340
Jensen H. Wong has resigned as chief executive, effective immediately, with an FBI investigation

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:39.080
in the background and his finance chief stepping in as interim CEO the chips keep shipping,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.200
but nobody outside yet knows what investigators are looking at inside the industry's main supplier.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.020
OpenAI's quarterly revenue has crossed $10 billion for the first time,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.060
even as it still spends far more than it brings in,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:01:02.360
And CoreWeave wants a $4 billion loan and is asking customers to pay up front, which could land on the startups renting its GPS.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.620
We'll also look at a seed round for physical, which builds data on where physical AI fails.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.020
Main stories first, then a few quick hits.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.900
First story and an update on one we've followed for three days now.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.840
What's new is that it's official.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:29.460
According to TechMeme, Jensen H. Wong has resigned as NVIDIA's CEO, president and board member, effective immediately.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.780
Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress is interim CEO while the board considers candidates.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.700
Ars Technica ties the board's urgent talks to an FBI investigation of NVIDIA.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.680
Gary Marcus relays a press report that a firing was coming.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:53.040
That report is unverified and his predictions about an anthropic IPO go beyond the evidence.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.940
The resignation itself is confirmed.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.480
Our read holds.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.700
A governance shock, not yet a supply shock.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.740
What matters is what the FBI is investigating and whether it touches export compliance or sales.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.000
Our second story is an update on OpenAI's revenue.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:27.480
TechCrunch relays a Blumerg report, citing anonymous sources that OpenAI's Q3 revenue crossed $10 billion, almost 50% above a reported $6.7 billion in Q2.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.280
Adjusted operating margin reportedly improved from 150% to 109%.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.500
The arithmetic checks, but nothing here shows OpenAI confirming the figures.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.060
We read the jump as a strong demand signal for the build-out.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.760
The test is whether margin keeps closing as prices fall.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:53.500
For early-stage companies, a supplier losing about $1.09 per revenue dollar

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:59.480
has reasons to raise a PI prices or move up stack, and app layer margins depend on which.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.800
The A.A. Realist view reads this as demand bought below cost.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.520
Until audited figures arrive, that view holds.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.120
The growth says more about pricing than about value delivered.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.300
From the company selling AI to a company renting out the hardware behind it.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.820
Third story, another update, CoreWeave.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:29.120
TechMeme reports it plans a $4 billion investment-grade loan at 7 to 7.5% interest,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.820
seeking cheaper money than its junk bonds.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:41.500
TechCrunch, reading its third-quarter results, says it is also moving customers to upfront payments and shorter contracts and could raise prices.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:48.240
The loan terms aren't verified beyond the headline, and the contract shift comes without specific data.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:56.000
Our view is that prepayments push CoreWeave's financing risk onto customers, spreading the debt load rather than shrinking it.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.860
For a seed company renting GPS, compute could turn from a monthly bill into upfront cash a seed round absorbs badly.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.120
The market reading is kinder.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.240
Better debt terms and prepaying customers look like maturation, not distress.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.680
The fourth and last of our main stories moves from who supplies compute to what physical AI systems learn from.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:26.520
In an announcement last Friday, Physical said it had raised a $10 million seed round.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.820
Inovia Capital led, joined by U.S. Venture Partners.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.060
Alongside the money, the company launched an app called Studio.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.380
It generates labeled scenarios from the physical world, specifically the ones where models fail.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:48.760
This is the company describing its own round and its own product, and its success metrics are self-reported.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.000
Checked against the evidence, the picture is mixed.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.980
The raise and the launch check out, but the one result offered on whether the product

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:01.740
works is unbenchmarked, and it was graded by the team of one of the company's own

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.360
advisors, which is not independent evidence.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:11.840
Our view is that labeled edge case scenarios are a credible wedge for physical AI.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.500
Rare failures are exactly what models miss, so data aimed at them goes after a real gap.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:22.700
we'd want to see is a lower failure rate on a customer's own system, graded by someone outside

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:28.420
the company's circle. Until then, the efficacy claim remains a claim. There's a wider point for

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:33.960
early-stage teams, too. If physicalized startups can buy edge-case data rather than build it,

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:40.740
that lowers a real cost. It also thins the moat for any team pitching its own proprietary failure

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:47.440
data as the advantage. A question worth putting to such a team might be what their data does

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.480
that a purchase set could not.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.320
Two quick ones before we close.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:01.220
A MD's latest quarterly filing shows it paid $2.9 billion for Talas, a company that builds

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.460
LLM weights directly into hardware.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:08.680
Our evidence check rates that unsupported, so it needs confirmation.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:13.220
If it holds, a MD is paying up for a different way to run models.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:20.060
And London-based Fractal AI has raised a $720 million Series C for an inference accelerator

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:23.980
aimed at reasoning and agentic workloads.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:29.640
Redpoint led, with Lightspeed co-leading and two others joining, it matters as another

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:35.940
well-funded alternative to NVIDIA's GPS in a field that also includes Cerebras.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.940
A question worth asking in diligence.

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:48.940
If your GPU provider demanded prepayment or raised prices next quarter, what's the plan and how many months of runway would it cost?

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:52.940
To watch. What the FBI is investigating at NVIDIA.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.940
Scope decides whether this stays governance or becomes supply risk.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:03.940
A DOJ filing, NVIDIA 8K, or credible report naming the subject would settle it.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:11.740
That's the briefing. Stay with me. Right after this, Bella takes you through today's deep dive

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:18.560
on repair or invention. An AI edits another model's weights. You can also read it or listen

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.820
to it on its own on the AI Briefing webpage, where you'll find the written edition in every

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.940
past episode. The link is in the show notes.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:35.500
Thanks, Michael.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:39.780
This is the AI Briefing Deep Dive for Thursday, November 12,

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:41.460
Repair or Invention.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.380
An AI edits another model's weights.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:45.220
I'm Bella.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:49.720
Like the briefing, it is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:08:00.280
On November 2, four researchers at MITCSail posted a write-up called Sparks of Weight Space Programming.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:08.340
They gave OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra small open model from Alibaba called QEN 3.50.8 billion.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.100
They asked Astra to make it better at math without training it.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:16.700
Astra changed 0.016% of the model's weights.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:25.620
On a standard math test called Math 500, the model's score went from 7.9% to 48.1%.

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:32.540
Three days later, Meter, an independent evaluation group, published an assessment it carried out inside Anthropic.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.840
Meter estimated that Anthropic's capability progress has roughly doubled in pace.

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:43.320
It put around 80% of that speed-up down to AI being used in the research itself.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:52.220
meanwhile anthropic launched its ipo roadshow seeking up to 88.5 billion dollar across the ipo

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.940
and a concurrent 26 billion dollar private placement that makes one question urgent

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:04.800
when we hear that ai is accelerating ai research how much is real improvement and how much is a

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:11.940
system getting better at the test it is graded on start with what a model is a language model

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.560
is a very large table of numbers called weights.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.000
This one has about 800 million of them.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.580
Normally, you improve a model by training it.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.100
You show it many examples,

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.800
and an automatic procedure called gradient descent

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.660
nudges every number a tiny amount toward fewer mistakes.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:29.560
Nobody decides what any single number should be.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.060
The MITCSAIL team had GPT-6 Astra

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:35.920
edit the weights of another model, QEN.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:38.960
Astra worked in a programming session

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.960
where it could read QEN's numbers directly,

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.360
change them, and run practice tests.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.140
The practice problems came from the training sets of two math collections,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.580
not from the test it would later be scored on.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.000
It could run up to 50 batches of practice.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:53.980
Astra soon noticed something.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:56.980
In 85 of 96 practice answers,

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.680
Quen ran until it hit its limit of 32,768 tokens,

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.560
which is roughly the length budget for one answer.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:09.520
The model was stuck in loops, repeating itself until it ran out of room.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:13.500
Astra traced the loop to one pattern written by two internal layers,

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:15.240
numbers 15 and 19.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.680
It subtracted that pattern from 64 columns in each of those layers.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:21.160
Here is an analogy.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:23.440
Picture a student who knows the material,

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:27.280
but has a nervous habit of rewriting the first line of every exam answer

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.600
until time runs out.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.840
The student fails, but not for lack of knowledge.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.680
A good tutor who spots the habit and breaks it will see the grade jump overnight.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.760
The tutor taught no new math.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.760
The authors make the same point about a second test.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:44.280
They tried the method on a different small model,

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.360
Google's Gemma 3 270 million, which rarely looped.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.180
Scores barely moved.

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.820
In the author's words, editing weights could remove an obstacle that hid an ability,

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:57.340
but it could not add an ability the model lacked.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:00.860
Here is what looks established.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:02.940
The scored tests were held out.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:05.600
According to a report carried on KuCoin,

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.840
Astra was given a set of math exercises to work from,

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:14.600
and the reported scores come from Math 500, GSM8K, and IFEVAL,

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:19.280
though the sources don't say whether those test problems were kept separate from the exercises.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.220
The gain also spread beyond math.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:26.680
On IFEVAL, a test of whether a model follows instructions,

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:31.420
the score went from 21.0% to 62.3%.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:34.260
That points to a general defect being removed,

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.040
not to answers being memorized.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.460
The result has also been rerun.

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:46.100
R&D World reports that a hugging face researcher got 51.0% on math 500 across 2,000 generations.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:50.480
The authors posted their full logs, now for what is not shown.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.980
The write-up does not mention explicit contamination checks,

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:59.380
meaning tests for whether the problems had leaked into the model's original training data.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.560
It is one small model, and the control model did not improve.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.700
It is a blog post, not peer-reviewed work.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.460
Critics also question how new the method is.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:15.000
Matthew Hannibal argues the post groups together experiments with very different tool access.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:22.180
Sean Godek describes Astra's editing steps as the same ones a human ML researcher would take.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:27.060
And because the edit targets reasoning loops that Quinn's own model card warns about,

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:31.660
our read is that Astra found a real fix, not a way to game the test.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:34.360
But it was a repair, not an invention.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:38.360
Meter's report raises a similar question in a different form.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:44.260
Meter's 1.9 times figure comes from an Anthropic index of benchmark scores,

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:48.960
with a confidence range of 1.5 times to 2.8 times.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.400
Anthropic was not paid, and it held redaction rights over the report.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:55.500
According to NextBigFuture,

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:59.740
Anthropic also redacted significant portions of the underlying evidence

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:01.260
before the report was published.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:05.580
Anthropic also canceled a planned check on how compute was allocated.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.220
Meter's own verdict is that the report does not achieve transparency about evidence.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:16.060
These facts support different readings.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:19.640
The AGI forward reading starts with something genuinely new.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:23.160
A general purpose model read another network's raw numbers,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.000
diagnosed a fault, and fixed it without any training.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.520
Add an outside estimate that AI is already speeding up a frontier lab,

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:35.380
and the loop people worry about, AI improving AI, looks less hypothetical.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:41.120
On this view, today's tiny repair is an early demonstration of a capability that will grow.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:44.660
The AI realist reading is just as reasonable.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:47.140
The fault was a known one, looping.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.180
The fix resembles a technique people already use.

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:53.080
It worked on one 0.8 billion weight model,

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:56.220
failed on the control model, and added no new ability.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:58.980
Meters number measures a benchmark index,

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:03.700
and the claim that AI caused the gain rests on what staff said about their own productivity.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.600
On this view, both results are ordinary progress told in the strongest possible terms.

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:14.820
The market reading cares less about which view is right and more about who is paying.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:22.120
The $26 billion private placement alongside Anthropix, IPO, comes from MD, NVIDIA,

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:27.340
TSMC and other suppliers whose sales depend on continued spending on AI.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:31.380
A story about accelerating research helps justify that spending.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:33.740
The policy reading looks at verification.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:39.000
This was the most in-depth outside access any lab has granted so far.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:43.280
Even so, the lab controlled what the public could see.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.740
If claims about AI speeding up its own research

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:50.000
start to shape regulation or securities disclosure,

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:53.060
auditors will need stronger access than this.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:58.520
our read is that this widens what can be built more than it speeds up the frontier

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:03.440
a frontier model can now find and fix some failures in a small open model directly

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:10.780
the mit run took 80 python executions over 20.3 hours that is cheap next to a fine-tuning project

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:15.780
though the cost of astra's own usage is not stated for companies serving small models

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:22.640
it suggests a new kind of repair fix loops refusals or formatting failures without retraining

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:27.300
The edge probably goes to teams with sharp failure diagnosis

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:30.260
And private evaluations that customers trust

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:35.000
The exposure falls on companies whose only product is tuning other people's models

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.220
Since a frontier model can increasingly do that job

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:41.740
One question worth exploring in diligence might be this

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.500
When a company says AI made its product better

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:47.380
Was that measured on data it never tuned against?

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:51.540
And did the improvement carry over to a second metric it was not aiming at?

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:57.080
The MIT teams, if eval result, is what a good answer looks like.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:00.680
Three things would settle this.

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:04.600
First, replication on larger models and on new kinds of failure.

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:08.380
If weight edits can add a capability rather than just remove an obstacle,

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:10.180
the realist reading weakens.

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:13.400
The authors say they plan standard benchmarks for this.

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:17.120
Second, a peer-reviewed version with contamination checks.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:20.520
Third, outside evidence on meters figure.

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:23.680
That could be Anthropic releasing the redacted material,

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:27.040
or a second auditor with full access to compute records.

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:32.760
Meter reviewed Anthropic's risk reports in February and again in August,

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.380
so the next round may come within months.

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:38.940
If the 1.9 times estimate holds up there,

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:41.940
that would matter more than any single clever edit.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:46.800
That's the deep dive and today's AI briefing.

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.540
Every source is linked in the show notes,

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:52.740
and the AI Briefing webpage has the written edition,

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:55.500
this deep dive and every past episode.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:57.960
The Daily Briefing is back tomorrow.
